      {
        "label": "A buck settles where its two areas balance, at Vout = D × Vin: 24 V at 50%, 12 V at 25% and 36 V at 75%",
        "ok": true
      },
      {
        "label": "A boost settles at Vin ÷ (1 − D): 96 V at 50% and 192 V at 75%. There 48 V for three quarters of a cycle and 144 V for a quarter both come to 36",
        "ok": true
      },
      {
        "label": "In a steady state, the average voltage across the inductor is zero: the drawn voltage averages zero over a cycle at every duty cycle, in both circuits",
        "ok": true,
        "source": "https://www.allpcb.com/allelectrohub/how-to-calculate-buck-converter-duty-cycle"
      },
      {
        "label": "The volt-second balance equation is (Vin − Vo) × Ton = Vo × Toff: it holds in Buck once settled, at every duty cycle and frequency",
        "ok": true,
        "source": "https://www.allpcb.com/allelectrohub/how-to-calculate-buck-converter-duty-cycle"
      },
      {
        "label": "Below the balance the current gains each cycle: with D at 50% and the output at 20 V, it climbs 2.8 A and falls 2.0 A, ending each cycle 0.8 A higher. Above it, it loses",
        "ok": true
      },
      {
        "label": "The output moves the way the bigger area says, at a rate that follows the difference: within 1% of a new setting in about 2 s of the slowed picture",
        "ok": true
      },
      {
        "label": "In Buck at 50%, the current rises 2.4 A a cycle at 50 kHz, 24% of 10 A; 1.2 A at 100 kHz, 12%; and 4.8 A at 25 kHz, 48%. The boost's 4.8 A on its 20 A average is also 24%",
        "ok": true
      },
      {
        "label": "A good estimation for the inductor ripple current is 20% to 40% of the output current: the band beside the ripple readout runs from 20% to 40%",
        "ok": true,
        "source": "https://www.ti.com/lit/an/slva477b/slva477b.pdf?ts=1786269682384"
      },
      {
        "label": "The battery current is the load's power over 48 V: 5.0 A at 50% and 2.5 A at 25% in Buck, 20.0 A at 50% and 40.0 A at 75% in Boost",
        "ok": true
      },
      {
        "label": "When the switch turns on in Boost, the voltage across the inductor increases to approximately the input voltage, and the current in it climbs",
        "ok": true,
        "source": "https://www.ti.com/download/trng/docs/seminar/Topic_3_Lynch.pdf"
      },
      {
        "label": "The boost converter is used to step up an input voltage to some higher level: its output sits above the battery at every duty cycle",
